.TH STRIP 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
strip \- remove unnecessary information from an object file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B strip
.RI [ \-o " strip_file" ]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B strip
removes symbol-table and debugging information from
.IR file ,
modifying it in place, when doing so can be proven safe; otherwise
.I file
is left byte-for-byte unchanged. Removal is never guaranteed to
happen \(em only that, if it happens, nothing needed to load or run
the file is disturbed.
.PP
Both ELF64 and PE object files are recognized, by their leading magic
bytes; any other file is refused with a diagnostic and neither read
further nor modified.
.SS "ELF64 objects"
Only
.B ET_EXEC
and
.B ET_DYN
files (an executable or a shared/position-independent object) with a
program header table are considered; a relocatable
.RB ( ET_REL )
object, or a file with no program headers, is left unchanged, since a
later link step may still need its symbol table. A section is a
candidate for removal only if its name is one of
.BR .symtab ", " .strtab ", " .symtab_shndx ", " .comment ","
.BR .gnu_debuglink ", " .gnu_debugdata ","
or begins with
.BR .debug_ ,
it carries no
.B SHF_ALLOC
flag (nothing a loader maps into memory), and its file content lies
entirely at or beyond the highest offset any program header actually
covers. The
.BR .dynsym / .dynstr
tables, and anything else a loaded segment might depend on, are never
removed. If any allocated section is found to lie at or beyond that
same offset boundary \(em meaning the "everything loaded lives below
this point" assumption does not hold for this particular file \(em the
entire strip is aborted and the file is left unchanged, rather than
risk a partial, unsafe rewrite. A section that would otherwise be
removed is kept instead if some other kept section still refers to it
by index, so removal never leaves a dangling reference.
.SS "PE objects"
Support is narrower and more conservative than for ELF, since this
build has no way to verify a stripped PE binary still runs correctly.
Two independent changes are made, each individually:
.IP \(bu 4
The legacy COFF symbol table is removed, but only when its byte range
\(em the fixed-size symbol records followed by a string table whose own
first four bytes give its total size \(em reaches exactly the end of
the file. If it does not reach exactly the end of the file, the
symbol table is left alone entirely.
.IP \(bu 4
The Debug Data Directory entry in the optional header, if present, is
zeroed. This clears a pointer/size pair inside the already-loaded
header region only; no section, RVA, or table elsewhere in the file
is moved or resized.
.PP
No PE section is ever removed (including an actual
.B .debug$
COFF section, if present) \(em doing so would require re-deriving
every relative virtual address that follows it, which cannot be
verified without a way to execute the result. The PE checksum field
is left stale after either change; ordinary loading does not validate
it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " strip_file"
Write the result to
.I strip_file
instead of modifying
.I file
in place. The output's permission bits are set to match
.IR file 's,
regardless of the current umask, since a stripped file is very often
still meant to be run as a program.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
The pathname of the object file to strip. Exactly one is accepted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Processing completed, whether or not anything was actually removed.
.TP
.B 1
.I file
could not be opened, read, or was not a regular file; the result
could not be written; or
.I file
was not a recognized ELF or PE object file.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error occurred: an invalid option, a missing operand, or more
than one operand.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nm (1),
.BR ar (1)
